# predictor-pruning chunk-2 A/B bench (issue #23, chunk 2)
# date:         2026-05-17T05:13Z (bench start) - 05:45Z (bench end)
# hardware:     AMD Ryzen 9 7950X (16C/32T water-cooled)
# build:        cargo build --release --features parallel-tree-learning
#               NO -C target-cpu=native (per CLAUDE.md benchmark rules)
# threads:      8 (RAYON_NUM_THREADS=8)
# samples:      8 paired iters per (image, effort) cell
# system load:  4-15 during bench (concurrent sibling agent running microbenches
#               in a separate workspace; mostly single-core noise that inflates
#               avg but spares min)
#
# A = baseline c579cbd1 (chunk-1 primitive shipped, NOT wired)
# B = this branch  (chunk-2: lb-skip wired into both sequential cfg paths)
#
# Method: paired interleaved A/B per iter; both binaries built with
# --features parallel-tree-learning so the only difference is the
# find_best_predictor body. Within each (image, effort) cell, A and B
# alternate iter-by-iter so thermal/turbo bias is paired out. Min is the
# preferred statistic because the avg is polluted by sibling-agent
# microbench bursts (visible as ~10-30% spikes on isolated iters).
#
# Profile images (real photos per CLAUDE.md no-synthetic rule):
#   0.26 MP: /home/lilith/work/codec-corpus/CID22/CID22-512/training/7256805.png
#   1.05 MP: /home/lilith/work/codec-corpus/clic2025-1024/02809272...png
#   4.19 MP: /home/lilith/work/codec-corpus/clic2025/final-test/8426ed22...png
#
# Byte-identity gate (per CLAUDE.md hash-lock discipline):
#   hash_lock_features --features parallel-tree-learning: 36/36 OK
#   hash_lock_features (no feature):                       36/36 OK
#   Direct sha256 check on 0.26 MP at e7/e8/e9 (real photo):
#     e7: d16f994ab0026a2a... (A == B)
#     e8: da397c48e41dca3f... (A == B)
#     e9: 642ecae8e60b3e10... (A == B)
#   Per-iter bytes column equality across all 9 cells × 8 iters: OK
#
# Results (min ms, paired Δ vs A):
#
#   image            eff    A_min    B_min    Δmin
#   ---------------------------------------------------
#   small_0.26MP     e7       270      269    -0.68%
#   small_0.26MP     e8       576      572    -0.79%
#   small_0.26MP     e9      1310     1307    -0.24%
#   medium_1.05MP    e7      1550     1545    -0.29%
#   medium_1.05MP    e8      2489     2470    -0.77%
#   medium_1.05MP    e9      3974     3815    -3.99%  ←  best cell
#   large_4.19MP     e7      3952     3952    -0.01%
#   large_4.19MP     e8      7684     7708    +0.32%
#   large_4.19MP     e9     20381    20531    +0.74%
#
# Headline:
#   - Hash-locks: BYTE-IDENTICAL across all 9 cells × 8 iters (verified).
#   - Brief target (≥3% on 1.05 MP @ e7): NOT MET (-0.29%).
#   - Only medium_1.05MP @ e9 clears 3% (-3.99% min).
#   - small / large at every effort, and medium at e7/e8: within ±1%
#     (signal indistinguishable from sibling-agent noise + thermal jitter).
#
# Root cause of the small e7 wall-clock delta:
#   The wired lb-skip lives in the SEQUENTIAL branches of
#   find_best_predictor (cfg gate + the small-range fallback at
#   range < PARALLEL_PRED_THRESHOLD=1024). Under
#   --features parallel-tree-learning, the lossless root call
#   (range = N samples, always >> 1024) takes the parallel branch
#   (compute_predictor_entropy fanned out via parallel_map) and lb-skip
#   never fires. The wireup is correct — it just targets paths that
#   are off the lossless hot path at the bench config:
#
#     * compute_best_tree (lossless): one root find_best_predictor call,
#       range = N → parallel branch → no lb-skip.
#     * compute_best_tree_with_multipliers (lossy modular only — LfFrame DC):
#       per-child find_best_predictor calls (tree_learn.rs:4065/4076) MAY
#       fall below the 1024 threshold and DO benefit. Not exercised by
#       the lossless bench config.
#     * non-parallel-tree-learning cfg path: any caller, any range → ALL
#       benefit. Not the default bench config.
#
# The medium e9 −3.99% min likely reflects deeper sub-1024 sub-tree calls
# at high effort (more nodes, more shallow per-child evals).
#
# Chunk 3 plan (to capture e7 wins):
#   Extend lb-skip to the parallel branch. Two viable shapes:
#     (a) Pre-compute all 14 LBs in parallel (cheap), sort ascending, then
#         sequentially evaluate in LB order, skipping any LB >= best-so-far.
#         Total work cap: 14 × LB + (14 × eval × (1 - prune_rate)).
#     (b) Atomic-shared best with per-task LB-then-eval; concurrent updates
#         allowed, strict-< tie-break via final scalar reduction (same
#         pattern as the existing parallel branch).
#   Option (a) is simpler and matches libjxl's "TryPredictor sorted by
#   lower bound" pattern; preferred. Microbench harness already exists
#   (predictor_prune_microbench.rs) — extend with a parallel variant.
#
# Reproduce:
#   SAMPLES=8 THREADS=8 ./scripts/bench_predictor_prune_ab.sh
#     > benchmarks/predictor_prune_ab_2026-05-17.tsv
#   python3 ./scripts/analyze_predictor_prune_ab.py benchmarks/predictor_prune_ab_2026-05-17.tsv
